<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Remote access (paired desktop) transmits plain ws:// to the paired computer. Android's default (API 28+) blocks that to
  arbitrary hosts, which broke pairing by raw Tailscale address in v1.1.x, so it is permitted at this level.

  Plain ws:// / http:// is still PERMITTED here, but the app refuses it everywhere except the
  phone itself and Tailscale (2026-10-30 security audit A1, Destin: "Tailscale only").

  Why this file cannot say that itself: Android's NetworkSecurityConfig <domain> only matches DNS
  hostnames. It has no CIDR/IP-range matching, and the pairing QR encodes a raw Tailscale address
  (100.64.0.0/10), so denying cleartext here would break every pairing. Where the rule is enforced
  instead, in code (all must agree):
    - renderer/remote-shim.ts connectToHost() and shared/cleartext-hosts.ts (the page, before it dials);
    - HostJsBridge "android:save-paired-device" (a computer is saved only at an allowed address);
    - WebViewUrlRouter (a download link over plain http only from an allowed address);
    - AppWebView.shouldInterceptRequest (any other plain http request the page makes is refused).
  The shared rule is CleartextPolicy.kt: loopback, 100.64.0.0/10, fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48 and *.ts.net.

  Tailscale users get WireGuard encryption under the plain WebSocket, and every connection is still
  gated by a bcrypt-verified password handshake. Play Store "Data safety": plain traffic now happens
  only to Tailscale/loopback addresses.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
